{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "sigiro-www",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-07-24",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],
  // No `main` and no assets directory. Blume finds this file at the project root
  // and hands it to `@astrojs/cloudflare` as `configPath`; the adapter generates
  // those values under `dist/server`. Naming the Alchemy entrypoint here breaks
  // the build because it imports that generated Worker.
  //
  // This file is build input, not a deployment entrypoint. Alchemy is the sole
  // deployment path and deploys `src/worker.ts`, which wraps Blume's generated
  // Worker with the apex auth and API proxies.
  //
  // The whole site, on one Worker. `sigiro.com/` is the landing page and
  // `sigiro.com/docs/*` is the docs, from one Blume app — so the `sigiro-docs`
  // Worker that served docs.sigiro.com for two days is gone, along with its custom
  // domain.
  //
  // Zone routes, not `custom_domain: true`. A custom domain makes Cloudflare own
  // the DNS record, so it refuses any hostname that already has one —
  // `Hostname 'sigiro.com' already has externally managed DNS records`. The
  // leftover proxied A records from the old Pages project are still there and
  // deleting them needs a DNS-edit credential this deploy path does not have. A
  // route needs no such thing: it intercepts the hostname at the edge ahead of
  // whatever those records point at.
  //
  // docs.sigiro.com is NOT here yet, and `src/middleware.ts` that redirects it is
  // therefore not reachable. Cloudflare's assets router answers before the Worker
  // runs, so a request to `docs.sigiro.com/quickstart` is a 404 from the asset
  // layer and the middleware never fires — verified against `wrangler dev`. The
  // knob for that is `assets.run_worker_first`, and this file cannot set it: the
  // adapter writes only `binding` and `directory` into the generated config and
  // drops everything else. It would also put every image and script through the
  // Worker, which is a poor trade for one redirect.
  //
  // So the subdomain stays on the old `sigiro-docs` Worker for now and is retired
  // separately.
  //
  // `workers_dev` is off: it published a second origin serving the identical site,
  // with our canonical tags, our sitemap and our OG tags on it.
  // This list carried five leading NEGATIVE rules, one per retired-URL redirect
  // under `/docs`. They are gone as of the vendored `156d5ff` build. The history
  // is worth keeping because it took three upstream changes to retire them.
  //
  // Blume derives `run_worker_first` from the first path segment of a content
  // route, so `/docs/reference` alone forces `/docs/*` — which also claims every
  // redirect under `/docs`. Inside a worker-first route the static layer never
  // runs, so the generated `_redirects` is never consulted and the Worker answers
  // instead. Two defects followed from that, fixed one at a time:
  //
  //   1. Astro's SSR handler defaulted a GET to a **301**, which browsers cache
  //      more or less forever. blume#170 (1.4.0) emitted these negatives itself;
  //      commit 2e231bc, also in 1.4.0, then replaced them with a redirect table
  //      baked into the generated wrapper Worker, which honours the configured
  //      status. A baked-in table costs nothing against Wrangler's caps of 100
  //      rules and 100 characters, so the list stops growing with every retired
  //      URL.
  //   2. That table answered with the bare destination and **dropped the query
  //      string**, silently breaking UTM and ref parameters on inbound links.
  //      Found here by measuring the removal rather than assuming it, filed as
  //      blume#175, fixed in 156d5ff — the wrapper now applies `_redirects`
  //      semantics: the request query is forwarded unless the destination carries
  //      its own, and a destination fragment stays last.
  //
  // Verified on the 156d5ff bundle with `wrangler dev`, then against production:
  //   /docs/api?utm_source=x&ref=y -> /docs/reference?utm_source=x&ref=y   (302)
  // which is what the asset layer did when the negatives were present.
  //
  // The positives stay. `/docs/tutorials/*`, `/docs/how-to/*` and
  // `/docs/explanation/*` are real content and MUST run worker-first, because
  // `Accept: text/markdown` negotiation on an extensionless URL happens in the
  // Worker. Excluding them would serve HTML to an agent that asked for Markdown.
  "assets": {
    "run_worker_first": [
      "/",
      "/docs",
      "/docs/*",
      "/.well-known/agent-configuration",
      "/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource",
      "/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/mcp",
      "/.well-known/mcp.json",
      "/.well-known/mcp/*",
      "/api/auth",
      "/api/auth/*",
      "/auth-ui.css",
      "/auth-ui.js",
      "/consent",
      "/device",
      "/health",
      "/healthz",
      "/ingest",
      "/ingest/*",
      "/openapi.json",
      "/push.v1.PusherService/Push",
      "/sign-in",
      "/sign-up",
      "/v1/*",
      "/mcp",
      "/v1development/profiles",
    ],
  },
  // No AUTH_ORIGIN_URL. `src/worker.ts` reaches auth through the `AUTH` service
  // binding that `alchemy.run.ts` wires to the auth Worker on D1; the
  // auth-origin.sigiro.com hostname and its tunnel are retired. Only
  // `src/worker-edge.ts` still takes a URL, and `scripts/check-edge.ts` passes
  // it with `--var` against `wrangler.edge-check.jsonc`.
  "vars": {
    "API_ORIGIN_URL": "https://api-origin.sigiro.com",
  },
  "workers_dev": false,
  "routes": [
    { "pattern": "sigiro.com/*", "zone_name": "sigiro.com" },
    { "pattern": "www.sigiro.com/*", "zone_name": "sigiro.com" },
  ],
  // Release artifacts, served by the `/dl/[...key]` route that
  // `blume.config.ts` injects.
  //
  // A bucket of its own, not `sigiro-prod`. That bucket holds tenant telemetry,
  // and /dl is an unauthenticated public route: putting both behind one binding
  // means one key-handling mistake in that route is a data leak rather than a
  // 404. Two buckets make that class of bug impossible instead of unlikely.
  //
  // The bucket itself stays private. Reads go through the Worker, so there is no
  // public bucket URL and no second hostname to add.
  "r2_buckets": [{ "binding": "DOWNLOADS", "bucket_name": "sigiro-dl" }],
}
